; Expected-failure: the result of a `throws` call is not handled.
;
; Until 2026-09-07 that was SILENTLY WRONG and thus worse than any other
; failure class around try: `let x step(b)` bound the result wrapper as the
; success value and delivered 6164131296 instead of the success case's 100. Nor
; did it propagate -- the same line in a function that ITSELF declares `throws`
; took the success arm, so that the error vanished between two functions
; that both say `throws`, and the caller with a proper
; `choose ... when e: Error` saw a success with a garbage value.
;
; The tree's convention is explicit propagation (throws_propagate.scaly:
; `choose` plus `throw e`); what was missing is that the omission said nothing.
;
; Six forms read the wrapper silently: the bare binding, `return f()`,
; `set x: f()`, a nested argument `g(f())`, the implicit
; return value and a condition `if f() > 0`. All six report now.
; xfail: the error of this throws call is not handled

define D(a: int)
define TErr union (Bad: D)

function step(b: int) returns int throws TErr
{
    if b = 0
        throw TErr.Bad(D(7))
    return b + 100
}

function main(argc: int, argv: pointer[pointer[const_char]]) returns int
{
    let x step(argc - 1)
    x
}
